    "type": "essay",
    "lecture": 0,
    "text": "Describe granule cells of cerebellar cortex.",
    "modelAnswer": "Small, densely packed neurons\nAxons ascend to molecular layer and form parallel fibers\nSynapse with Purkinje dendrites\nDendrites form claw-like endings in glomeruli",
    "keyConcept": "Granule cells of the cerebellar cortex are small, densely packed neurons that synapse with Purkinje cell dendrites.",
    "explanation": "Granule cells are the most abundant type of neuron in the cerebellar cortex and play a crucial role in the cerebellum's function, particularly in motor coordination and learning. Their axons, known as parallel fibers, ascend to the molecular layer and form synapses with the dendrites of Purkinje cells, which are the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex. The unique structure of granule cell dendrites, which form claw-like endings in glomeruli, allows for efficient synaptic transmission and integration of signals.",
    "tags": []
