    "keyConcept": "The gracile and cuneate nuclei are two dorsal column nuclei in the medulla that receive sensory input from the body and play a crucial role in transmitting this information to the brain.",
    "explanation": "The gracile and cuneate nuclei are located in the dorsal part of the medulla and receive sensory input from the lower and upper body, respectively, via the dorsal columns of the spinal cord. This input is then transmitted to the brain via the medial lemniscus, a pathway that is essential for the transmission of proprioceptive and tactile information. Understanding the role of these nuclei is critical for appreciating the neural mechanisms underlying sensory perception and motor control.",
